---
import Video from '../../components/story/Video.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/story.css';
---
<Base wide title="Matrix for Claude Code: less than half the time, a fifth of the tokens" image="/og-matrix-claude-code.png" description="A free plugin that gives Claude Code a local, searchable index of PDFs, Word files, scans and charts. Measured on 442 pages: 20 instead of 43 seconds per question, a fifth of the input tokens.">
<div class="story">

  <section class="beat" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="in">
      <Video src="/video/matrix-claude-code-1080p.mp4" poster="/video/matrix-claude-code-poster.jpg" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Matrix for Claude Code</p>
      <h1>Claude Code needed less than half the time and a fifth of the tokens.</h1>
      <p class="big">I measured Claude Code with and without this free plugin on 442 pages of EU regulations and a report. With it, a
      question took 20 instead of 43 seconds on average and 182k instead of 922k input tokens. Both got nearly
      every answer right; the one wrong answer, without the plugin, came from a single run.</p>
      <p class="muted">The plugin gives Claude Code a local index of your PDFs, Word files, scans and charts. Every answer
      comes with file, page and passage. Claude Code is the language model, so you need no extra model and no API key.</p>
      <a class="go" href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp">Get it on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">The problem</p><h2>Without an index, every question starts from zero.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <p class="big">Claude Code converts the PDFs to text, greps, opens pages and greps again. That works, but on a few
        hundred pages it takes about a dozen tool calls per question.</p>
        <p class="muted">Matrix reads the documents once. It splits them by their structure, indexes them and gives
        Claude Code short excerpts of the matching sections instead of whole documents.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">What it does</p>
      <h2>It finds the passage and the page.</h2>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Answers with sources</h3><p>Every answer names file, page and passage. The citations are checked against the original text.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Tables across documents</h3><p>One question for every document or passage, returned as a table with a source in each cell.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Other words, other languages</h3><p>Search works on meaning, so an English question finds the German provision.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Scans and charts</h3><p>Scanned pages are searchable. A value that exists only in a chart is found on its page.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Cross-references</h3><p>"Pursuant to Article 26" leads to Article 26, and Claude can read the whole article, not just a snippet.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Stays current</h3><p>New and changed files in the folder are indexed again automatically. Deleted files drop out of the search.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The measurement</p>
      <h2>Two test sets, all public.</h2>
      <p class="big">Claude Code alone against Claude Code with the plugin, on public EU regulations and reports.
      Same questions, same prompt. I wrote down the right answers before the first run.</p>
      <div class="numbers">
        <div><strong>20 s</strong><span>average time per question on 442 pages, instead of 43 s without the plugin</span></div>
        <div><strong>182k</strong><span>average input tokens per question, instead of 922k</span></div>
        <div><strong>7.9</strong><span>average tool calls per question, instead of 12.8</span></div>
        <div><strong>−34 %</strong><span>input tokens on 1,592 pages in 14 documents and four languages; 35 % less time. Measured in October with version 0.2.3</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="demo">
          <thead><tr><th>Question (442 pages)</th><th>Alone</th><th>With Matrix</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>English question, German legal text</td><td>34 s · 665k tokens</td><td class="ok">21 s · 172k tokens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A number that exists only in a chart</td><td>38 s · 837k tokens</td><td class="ok">21 s · 232k tokens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>One answer spread over three regulations</td><td>66 s · 1,377k tokens</td><td class="ok">27 s · 223k tokens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Paraphrased question, single regulation</td><td>35 s · 808k tokens</td><td class="ok">12 s · 102k tokens</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem">Averages per run, Claude Code 2.1.309 with Sonnet and plugin 0.2.9, November 2026.
      Two runs per question with the plugin, one without. Input tokens include cache reads, which make up most of them. The questions, the right answers
      and the script to run it again are in <a href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp/blob/main/DEMO.md">DEMO.md</a>.</p>
      <p class="muted">The first run of this test, in October with Claude Code 2.1.222 and an earlier plugin version, gave 42
      instead of 126 seconds and 303k instead of 651k tokens. Without the plugin, Claude Code now needs 43 instead of 126
      seconds, but more input tokens, 922k instead of 651k. So the plugin now saves a bit more than half the
      time instead of two thirds, and four fifths of the tokens instead of about half.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The limits</p>
      <h2>It saves time and tokens. Better answers are not proven.</h2>
      <p class="big">Claude Code alone got 3 of 4 answers right. One wrong answer in one run is not an error rate.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>More documents, more gain</h3><p>The gain is largest when the question uses other words than the text (here 35 → 12 s). For one short document it is small.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Spread-out questions: mixed</h3><p>On the larger set, in October with version 0.2.3, a question across three acts saved almost nothing, because Claude still searches once per act. On the 442 pages in November it was the other way round: 66 → 27 s.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Indexing takes minutes</h3><p>Once per folder: 16 minutes for 1,592 pages, with no Claude tokens. It pays off if you ask more than once.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Page numbers slipped</h3><p>In the October test of version 0.2.3, two citations were one page off where a passage crosses a page break. File, article and passage were right in every answer. Fixed in the released 0.2.3: a hit lists every page its passage covers.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Charts are easy to misread</h3><p>Without the plugin, Claude Code took 15.87 % from another chart (p. 13) in a low-resolution page image, instead of 19.01 % on p. 10. In October the plugin had the same problem once, because the page image was too small. This time it found and read the chart page right in both runs.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Small samples</h3><p>Two runs per question with the plugin and one without on 442 pages in November, one run on 1,592 pages in October. Differences of 10–20 % between runs are normal.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
      <h2>Code finds the passages. Claude reads them.</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><div><h3>Split along the real structure <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>By PDF layout, headings, articles and tables. No language model decides where a section ends. Scans and charts are indexed too.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Search by meaning and by keyword <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>Vectors from a multilingual embedding model plus full-text search, merged by rank. The vector database is built in and runs on SQLite: nothing to install, no server. An optional second model re-sorts the top hits.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Short hits <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>A search returns as many short excerpts as fit into one result, each with file, pages and the path document › chapter › article.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Read before citing <span class="who ai">AI</span></h3><p>Claude Code opens the passage or the article it wants to quote. Then it answers with file, page and passage.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Check the citations <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>Each citation is checked against the original text.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">Installation</p><h2>Two commands, then ask.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <div class="card"><pre><code>claude plugin marketplace add matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp
claude plugin install matrix@matrix-mcp</code></pre></div>
        <p class="muted">Start Claude Code in the folder with your documents and ask. The first time, it walks you
        through the setup. It runs on macOS (Apple silicon), Windows 10 or later (x64) and Linux (x64 and arm64), with Node.js 18 or later.
        If it does not run on your system, <a href="mailto:matthias.nordwig@programmiere.de?subject=Matrix%20for%20Claude%20Code">tell me</a>.</p>
        <p class="muted">Matrix reads the folder you started in and asks before it opens any other. It never changes or
        deletes your files. The only text that leaves your computer is what Claude Code reads anyway.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in read">
      <p class="kicker">Where it comes from</p>
      <h2>It started as an app.</h2>
      <p class="big">The plugin runs on the same Rust core as <a href="/projects/matrix/">Matrix</a>, my document app for Mac,
      Windows and iPhone. In the app a local model does the reading; here Claude Code does it.</p>
      <a class="go" href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp">Get it on GitHub</a>
      <a class="go ghost" href="mailto:matthias.nordwig@programmiere.de?subject=Matrix%20for%20Claude%20Code">Write to me</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
</Base>
